{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-94062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $U$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $V$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ and $U$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-94062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$T$ and $U$ only"] ["text" "$T$ and $V$ only"] ["text" "$T$ only"] ["text" "$U$ and $V$ only"] ["text" "$U$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$T$ and $U$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $Q$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $R$ is not symmetric since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-93700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$P$ and $Q$ only"] ["text" "$P$ and $R$ only"] ["text" "$P$ only"] ["text" "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$P$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ has an associated $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $D$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}.$ Although it's a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $D$ is not symmetric since ${d}_{24}\\ne {d}_{42}.$\n\n- $E$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $4\\times 4$).\n\n- $F$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it is a $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$F$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-94063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D$ and $F$ only"] ["text" "$D$ only"] ["text" "$E$ and $F$ only"] ["text" "$E$ only"] ["text" "$F$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$F$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-140870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-140870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-140870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-140870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-140870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$R$ and $S$ only"] ["text" "$R$ only"] ["text" "$S$ and $T$ only"] ["text" "$S$ only"] ["text" "$T$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-140870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$T$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $K$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $L$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since ${m}_{12}\\ne {m}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$K$ and $L$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-94059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$K$ and $L$ only"] ["text" "$K$ and $M$ only"] ["text" "$K$ only"] ["text" "$L$ and $M$ only"] ["text" "$L$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$K$ and $L$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-94086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "41"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-4)+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+0+16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-93703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "29"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-93703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "29"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.json" "file_sha256" "47eb17e72a309bc7258c893e6a58dd46ef8ba48be40978e9fbd5fd00f5ae9b11"} {"question" "q-94087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "99f3570307c15d08fb4264489cfdb2ce62346d9c0518ce443da145d907533d92"} {"question" "q-94087" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14051940/state.jso