"}] "usage" {"q-93700" [] "e-8169" [] "e-8171" [] "e-8170" [] "q-93703" [] "e-8168" []} "basis" 1754 "retractions" [[17592186162518 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $R^{2}$?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186162518 :question/answer-fields 17592186160740] [17592186162519 :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form\n\n$Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} - 3x_{1}x_{3} - x_{2}x_{3} + x_{3}^{2}$.\nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186161708 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186161708 :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}$.\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\""] [17592186161709 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186161709 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x^{T}Ax \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 23\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 86\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161710 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186161710 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term $a_{ij}x_i x_j$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= -1x_{1}x_{1} + (1)x_{1}x_{2} + (1)x_{2}x_{1} + - 1x_{2}x_{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}x_{1} - x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ x_{1} - x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{1}(-x_{1} + x_{2}) + x_{2}(x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}x_{1} - x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161711 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & 2 & c \\\\ b & c & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1^2 - 2x_1x_3 + 4x_2x_3 + 2x_2^2$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c$?"] [17592186161711 :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{3} + 4x_{2}x_{3} + 2x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1x_{1}^{2} + 0x_{1}x_{2} + (-1)x_{1}x_{3} \\\\\n&= + 0x_{2}x_{1} + 2x_{2}^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= + (-1)x_{3}x_{1} + 2x_{3}x_{2} + 0x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ${\\color{blue}-2}x_1x_3$ between the entries $a_{13}=a_{31}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-2}{2}=-1$, and\n- we split the coefficient of ${\\color{blue}4}x_2x_3$ between the entries $a_{23}=a_{32}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{2}=2$.\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$, $b=-1$, and $c=2$. Finally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2 = 2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186160741 17592186160742 17592186160743 17592186160744 17592186160745 17592186160747 17592186160748 17592186160749 17592186160750 17592186160751] "immutable_fields" [[17592186160740 :answer-field/correct] [17592186160740 :answer-field/id] [17592186160740 :answer-field/key] [17592186160740 :answer-field/type] [17592186160746 :answer-field/correct] [17592186160746 :answer-field/id] [17592186160746 :answer-field/key] [17592186160746 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
